Games-Sokoban-Solver version 0.01
=================================

A solver for Sokoban levels, written in Perl.  A level in the standard text
format (the characters # $ . @ * +) is read, reduced to what matters for the
search, analysed, and solved twice: first by a race of twenty-seven search
modes of four engines -- the first mode to find a solution wins and the others
stop -- and then by an optimal solver that looks for the shortest solution and
proves it where it can.  The program bin/soko-solver.pl prints the solution as
a path in the letters u U r R l L d D (a capital letter is a push).

This is the solver pipeline of the Sokoban project of Jiri Broz, transplanted
into a distribution so that it can be installed and run anywhere.  The project
itself keeps a database of 32 000 levels and their solutions; nothing of that
database is needed here.

USAGE

   soko-solver.pl levels.xsb
   soko-solver.pl --time 120 --optimal 300 --jobs 8 --format report levels.xsb
   perldoc soko-solver.pl

One line per level on standard output: the solution, or the word UNSOLVED or
REFUSED; the account of the run on standard error.  The first run asks a few
questions (threads, seconds for the race and the proof, a memory ceiling) and
writes them to a settings file; every setting is an option as well.

A level takes at most about

   modes x time / jobs + the analyses + 2 x optimal   seconds

(27 modes, 60 s each, 4 threads, 60 s for the proof: about ten minutes at
the worst; most levels of ordinary size are solved in seconds).  The deadlock
patterns of a level are computed when it is solved, under a budget of 30 s by
default; the Sokoban project spent a year of processor time on its table, so a
search here may take another way or fail where that project's campaign
succeeded.

From Perl: perldoc Games::Sokoban::Solver.

INSTALLATION

To install this module type the following:

   perl Makefile.PL
   make
   make test
   make install

On Windows with Strawberry Perl use gmake in place of make.

A perl with 64-bit integers is required (every perl built in the last two
decades has them; Makefile.PL refuses otherwise).

DEPENDENCIES

One module outside the perl core:

  Array::Heap   the priority queue of the first-solution search

Optional, used when present:

  BerkeleyDB                           search stores on disk for long runs
  Win32::API, Win32::Console,          console width, colours and the memory
  Win32::Console::ANSI                 probe on Windows

